@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Managing Branches";
}

<main id="tutorials" class="container py-1">
    <div class="row g-4">

        <!-- Sidebar -->
        <aside class="col-lg-3">
            @Html.Partial("~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubTopics.cshtml")
        </aside>

        <!-- Main Content -->
        <section class="col-lg-9">
            <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
                <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

                    <h1 class="fw-bold mb-3">Managing Branches in Git</h1>

                    <p class="lead text-muted">
                        Learn how to create, view, rename, and delete
                        Git branches while keeping your project organized.
                    </p>

                    <hr class="my-4" />

                    <h3 class="fw-bold">1. What Is Branch Management?</h3>

                    <p>
                        Branch management is the process of organizing
                        different lines of development in a Git repository.
                    </p>

                    <p>
                        Developers use branches to work on new features,
                        fix bugs, and test changes without directly
                        modifying the main branch.
                    </p>

                    <div class="alert alert-info">
                        <strong>Example:</strong>
                        In a Student Management System, you can create
                        separate branches for login, registration,
                        and payment functionality.
                    </div>

                    <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">2. View Existing Branches</h3>

                    <p>
                        Use the following command to display local branches:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git branch</code></pre>

                    <p>Example output:</p>

                    <pre class="bg-light border p-3 rounded"><code>  feature-login
* main
  payment-module</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        The <code>*</code> symbol indicates the
                        currently selected branch.
                    </p>

                    <h5 class="fw-semibold mt-4">View Local and Remote Branches</h5>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git branch -a</code></pre>

                    <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">3. Create a New Branch</h3>

                    <p>
                        To create a branch without switching to it:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git branch feature-login</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        This creates a branch named
                        <code>feature-login</code>.
                    </p>

                    <div class="alert alert-warning">
                        <strong>Important:</strong>
                        Creating a branch with <code>git branch</code>
                        does not automatically switch to it.
                    </div>

                    <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">4. Create and Switch to a Branch</h3>

                    <p>
                        The recommended modern Git command is:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git switch -c feature-registration</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Alternatively, you can use:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git checkout -b feature-registration</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Both commands create a new branch and
                        immediately switch to it.
                    </p>

                    <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">5. Switch Between Existing Branches</h3>

                    <p>Switch to the main branch:</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git switch main</code></pre>

                    <p>Switch to a feature branch:</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git switch feature-login</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Git may prevent switching if your uncommitted
                        changes would be overwritten. Commit, stash,
                        or safely discard those changes first.
                    </p>

                    <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">6. Rename a Branch</h3>

                    <p>Rename the current branch:</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git branch -m new-branch-name</code></pre>

                    <p>Rename another local branch:</p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git branch -m old-branch-name new-branch-name</code></pre>

                    <div class="alert alert-secondary">
                        <strong>Note:</strong>
                        Renaming a local branch does not automatically
                        rename the corresponding branch on GitHub.
                    </div>

                    <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">7. Delete a Branch</h3>

                    <p>
                        Delete a local branch after its changes
                        have been merged:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git branch -d feature-login</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        Force-delete a local branch:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git branch -D feature-login</code></pre>

                    <div class="alert alert-danger">
                        <strong>Warning:</strong>
                        The <code>-D</code> option can delete a branch
                        even if its changes have not been merged.
                        Use it carefully.
                    </div>

                    <p>
                        You cannot delete the branch currently checked out
                        in your working tree. Switch to another branch first.
                    </p>

                    <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">8. Push a Branch to GitHub</h3>

                    <p>
                        After creating and committing changes on
                        a feature branch, publish it to GitHub:
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code>git push -u origin feature-login</code></pre>

                    <p>
                        The <code>-u</code> option sets the upstream
                        tracking relationship, making future
                        push and pull commands easier.
                    </p>

                    <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">9. Practical Example</h3>

                    <p>
                        Suppose you are developing a Login Module
                        for a Student Management System.
                    </p>

                    <pre class="bg-dark text-light p-3 rounded"><code># Switch to main
git switch main
















# Create a new feature branch
git switch -c feature-login

# Check the active branch
git branch

# Stage your changes
git add .

# Commit your work
git commit -m "Add student login module"

# Push the branch to GitHub
git push -u origin feature-login</code></pre>

                            <p>
                                You can now create a pull request on GitHub
                                    to review and merge the login feature.
                                    </p>

                                <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">10. Common Branch Commands</h3>

                                    <div class="table-responsive">
                                <table class="table table-bordered table-striped align-middle">
                                <thead class="table-dark">
                                    <tr>
                                    <th>Command</th>
                                <th>Purpose</th>
                                </tr>
                                    </thead>
                                    <tbody>
                                <tr>
                                <td><code>git branch</code></td>
                                    <td>List local branches</td>
                                    </tr>
                                <tr>
                                <td><code>git branch -a</code></td>
                                    <td>List local and remote-tracking branches</td>
                                    </tr>
                                <tr>
                                <td><code>git branch feature</code></td>
                                    <td>Create a branch</td>
                                    </tr>
                                <tr>
                                <td><code>git switch feature</code></td>
                                    <td>Switch to a branch</td>
                                    </tr>
                                <tr>
                                <td><code>git switch -c feature</code></td>
                                    <td>Create and switch to a branch</td>
                                    </tr>
                                <tr>
                            <td><code>git branch -m new-name</code></td>
                        <td>Rename the current branch</td>
                    </tr>
                                <tr>
                    <td><code>git branch -d feature</code></td>
                                    <td>Safely delete a merged branch</td>
                    </tr>
                        <tr>
                        <td><code>git branch -D feature</code></td>
                        <td>Force-delete a branch</td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                        <td><code>git push -u origin feature</code></td>
                    <td>Publish a branch and set upstream</td>
                                </tr>
                    </tbody>
                        </table>
                        </div>

                        <h3 class="fw-bold mt-5">11. Best Practices</h3>

                    <ul>
                        <li>Use meaningful branch names such as <code>feature-login</code>.</li>
                    <li>Keep the main branch stable.</li>
                    <li>Create separate branches for different features.</li>
                        <li>Commit changes regularly with clear messages.</li>
                        <li>Review changes before merging branches.</li>
                            <li>Delete old branches after confirming they are no longer needed.</li>
                            </ul>

                    <div class="alert alert-success mt-4">
                        <strong>Key Takeaway:</strong>
                            Git branch management allows developers to
                            organize features, isolate changes, and
                        collaborate efficiently without disrupting
                        the main codebase.
                    </div>

